 ## tab-keys.mjs
 
-### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab never renumbers the others
-- Since: 2026-09-02
-- Decision: a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`).
-- Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them contiguous.
-- Reason: the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right of the closed one
-  and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the "close keeps keys" test).
-- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs`
+### §stable-tab-keys — closing a tab renumbers the others to stay contiguous (reversed 2026-10-23)
+- Since: 2026-10-23 (reverses the 2026-09-02 rule that closing a tab never renumbers the others)
+- Decision: `closeTab` renumbers the remaining tabs `t1..tn` in their current order and sets `next` to `n + 1`, so
+  keys stay contiguous. Closing `t2` from `t1,t2,t3,t4` leaves `t1,t2,t3` (the old `t3` becomes `t2`, the old `t4`
+  becomes `t3`). A tab opened after a close gets the next contiguous key. A tab's title moves with it to its new key.
+- Reversed: 2026-10-23 by the maintainer, who named this decision and explicitly overrode it.
+- Risk: the key is the React key and the URL state, so renumbering can bring back the remount problem below for
+  every tab to the right of the closed one: scroll position and unsaved form input are lost. No mitigation (such as a
+  separate stable React id) was added; that was outside what the maintainer asked for.
+- Previous decision (2026-09-02 to 2026-10-23, history): a key is assigned once from `next` and is never reused or
+  shifted. Closing a tab leaves a gap (`t1,t3`). Forbidden: renumbering the remaining keys after a close to make them
+  contiguous.
+- Previous reason (history): the key is the React key and the URL state. Shifting keys remounts every tab to the right
+  of the closed one and drops its scroll position and unsaved form input (incident 2026-08-29, reproduced by the old
+  "close keeps keys" test).
+- Evidence: `tab-keys.test.mjs` (the old "close keeps keys" test was rewritten to assert contiguous keys).
